INSERT INTO sat_questions (question_id, external_id, vault_id, disclosed_item_id, source, program, test_type, test_type_desc, domain, primary_class_cd, skill_cd, skill_desc, difficulty, score_band_range_cd, question_type, stimulus_html, stem_html, answer_options, correct_answer, answer_keys, rationale_html, correct_answer_extracted_from_rationale, image_count, parent_template_name, parent_template_id, template_cluster_id, template_cluster_name, origin, position, answer_style, section, raw_payload, created_date, updated_date) VALUES
    ($SATQ$aaddd60f$SATQ$, $SATQ$d1efe6e8-5201-4a69-97dd-f475820fb912$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$4f07e270-9128-45a5-8ffa-865800d33eeb$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$INF$SATQ$, $SATQ$Inferences$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Scientists studying Mars long thought the history of its crust was relatively simple. One reason for this is that geologic and climate data collected by a spacecraft showed that the crust was largely composed of basalt, likely as a result of intense volcanic activity that brought about a magma ocean, which then cooled to form the planet&rsquo;s surface. A study led by Valerie Payr&eacute; focused on additional information&mdash;further analysis of data collected by the spacecraft and infrared wavelengths detected from Mars&rsquo;s surface&mdash;that revealed the presence of surprisingly high concentrations of silica in certain regions on Mars. Since a planetary surface that formed in a mostly basaltic environment would be unlikely to contain large amounts of silica, Payr&eacute; concluded that <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"22c694b7-ab8d-4454-953b-f1f0ce77ae11","content":"<p>the information about silica concentrations collected by the spacecraft is likely more reliable than the silica information gleaned from infrared wavelengths detected from Mars&rsquo;s surface.</p>"},{"id":"701e2b9c-1489-4b0a-89db-3acddda13404","content":"<p>high silica concentrations on Mars likely formed from a different process than that which formed the crusts of other planets.</p>"},{"id":"dfdc5723-7fc8-4258-a670-00f771527df4","content":"<p>having a clearer understanding of the composition of Mars&rsquo;s crust and the processes by which it formed will provide more insight into how Earth&rsquo;s crust formed. &nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"98db816e-1b69-4741-a6c5-94f805b3d5ee","content":"<p>Mars&rsquo;s crust likely formed as a result of other major geological events in addition to the cooling of a magma ocean.&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["98db816e-1b69-4741-a6c5-94f805b3d5ee"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. Cooling magma would create basalt, but &ldquo;a planetary surface that formed in a mostly basaltic environment would be unlikely to contain large amounts of silica.&rdquo; Since Mars&rsquo;s crust does contain large amounts of silica, it is unlikely that Mars&rsquo;s crust was formed exclusively by cooling magma. Therefore, there were likely other major geological events that created the high silica concentrations. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although the passage discusses these two methods of collecting data about Mars&rsquo;s crust, it never compares their reliability, so there&rsquo;s no basis for this inference. Choice B is incorrect. The passage never mentions anything about the crusts of other planets, so there&rsquo;s no basis for this inference. Choice C is incorrect. The passage never mentions Earth&rsquo;s crust, so there&rsquo;s no basis for this inference. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-INF-H009$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$8c439a6b-bad8-41ab-baeb-c36e23b3f3bd$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"aaddd60f","external_id":"d1efe6e8-5201-4a69-97dd-f475820fb912","vaultid":"4f07e270-9128-45a5-8ffa-865800d33eeb","uId":"4938eb66-5f57-4083-addd-331a449e148e","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"INF","skill_desc":"Inferences","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Scientists studying Mars long thought the history of its crust was relatively simple. One reason for this is that geologic and climate data collected by a spacecraft showed that the crust was largely composed of basalt, likely as a result of intense volcanic activity that brought about a magma ocean, which then cooled to form the planet&rsquo;s surface. A study led by Valerie Payr&eacute; focused on additional information&mdash;further analysis of data collected by the spacecraft and infrared wavelengths detected from Mars&rsquo;s surface&mdash;that revealed the presence of surprisingly high concentrations of silica in certain regions on Mars. Since a planetary surface that formed in a mostly basaltic environment would be unlikely to contain large amounts of silica, Payr&eacute; concluded that <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"22c694b7-ab8d-4454-953b-f1f0ce77ae11","content":"<p>the information about silica concentrations collected by the spacecraft is likely more reliable than the silica information gleaned from infrared wavelengths detected from Mars&rsquo;s surface.</p>"},{"id":"701e2b9c-1489-4b0a-89db-3acddda13404","content":"<p>high silica concentrations on Mars likely formed from a different process than that which formed the crusts of other planets.</p>"},{"id":"dfdc5723-7fc8-4258-a670-00f771527df4","content":"<p>having a clearer understanding of the composition of Mars&rsquo;s crust and the processes by which it formed will provide more insight into how Earth&rsquo;s crust formed. &nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"98db816e-1b69-4741-a6c5-94f805b3d5ee","content":"<p>Mars&rsquo;s crust likely formed as a result of other major geological events in addition to the cooling of a magma ocean.&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["98db816e-1b69-4741-a6c5-94f805b3d5ee"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. Cooling magma would create basalt, but &ldquo;a planetary surface that formed in a mostly basaltic environment would be unlikely to contain large amounts of silica.&rdquo; Since Mars&rsquo;s crust does contain large amounts of silica, it is unlikely that Mars&rsquo;s crust was formed exclusively by cooling magma. Therefore, there were likely other major geological events that created the high silica concentrations. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although the passage discusses these two methods of collecting data about Mars&rsquo;s crust, it never compares their reliability, so there&rsquo;s no basis for this inference. Choice B is incorrect. The passage never mentions anything about the crusts of other planets, so there&rsquo;s no basis for this inference. Choice C is incorrect. The passage never mentions Earth&rsquo;s crust, so there&rsquo;s no basis for this inference. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-INF-H009","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"8c439a6b-bad8-41ab-baeb-c36e23b3f3bd","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959368,"updateDate":1691007959368}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959368, 1691007959368),
    ($SATQ$ab94d40a$SATQ$, $SATQ$467ea699-a005-4116-b3c0-6ccb84420217$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$5deb4b09-51a2-4780-bb92-7a97ae4247c3$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><figure class="table"><table class="gdr"><caption style="caption-side: top;"><p style="text-align: center;">Time Participants Spent Reading about Five London Museums</p></caption><thead><tr><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Museum Name</th><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Ranking</th><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Percentage of total time spent reading about museum by participants provided with ranking</th><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Percentage of total time spent reading about museum by participants not provided with ranking</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">British Museum</th><td style="text-align: center;">1</td><td style="text-align: center;">36</td><td style="text-align: center;">18</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">National Gallery</th><td style="text-align: center;">2</td><td style="text-align: center;">21</td><td style="text-align: center;">20</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">Tate Modern</th><td style="text-align: center;">4</td><td style="text-align: center;">16</td><td style="text-align: center;">17</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">Victoria and Albert Museum</th><td style="text-align: center;">5</td><td style="text-align: center;">14</td><td style="text-align: center;">23</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">Natural History Museum</th><td style="text-align: center;">3</td><td style="text-align: center;">13</td><td style="text-align: center;">22</td></tr></tbody></table></figure></p>
<p>Researchers recently conducted an experiment to understand how we use rankings to make decisions. They created a fictitious travel website describing five museums in London. Then, they invited two groups of participants, who had never visited the museums, to review the site and select the museum they would be most likely to visit. Meanwhile, the researchers tracked the amount of time each participant spent reading about each museum. For one group, the website ranked each museum, titling the page &ldquo;The Top 5 Museums in London.&rdquo; For the other group, the museums and their descriptions were not ranked. The researchers concluded that when reviewing ranked lists, we tend to focus on the top-ranked option.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes data in the table that support the researchers&rsquo; conclusion?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"430e6af9-2b1b-4417-a771-033acc192aa8","content":"<p>Participants who were not provided with a ranking of the museums spent roughly equal amounts of time reading about each museum.</p>"},{"id":"6dd4d30b-e3c7-4c79-aaca-11339b460705","content":"<p>Participants who were provided with a ranking of the museums spent disproportionately more time reading about the British museum.</p>"},{"id":"79ad97da-b8af-4bb4-9a6a-0fe6f3816870","content":"<p>Participants who were provided with a ranking of the museums spent the least amount of time reading about the Natural History Museum.</p>"},{"id":"df2c3610-ed05-4db8-81ba-7c9f1b946042","content":"<p>Participants who were not provided with a ranking of the museums spent the most time reading about the Victoria and Albert Museum.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["6dd4d30b-e3c7-4c79-aaca-11339b460705"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. By looking at the top-ranked option, we can see that people provided with ranked lists spent more time reading about the British Museum than reading about other museums (36% of the time versus 21% for the second-ranked option).</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The claim is about people with ranked lists, and these data are about those with unranked lists. Choice C is incorrect. The claim is about people with ranked lists looking at the top-ranked option, and these data are about the third-ranked option. Choice D is incorrect. The claim is about people with ranked lists, and these data are about those with unranked lists.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-QUA-E005$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$54d406f8-0ad6-47ab-8e00-4a071b0e2187$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"ab94d40a","external_id":"467ea699-a005-4116-b3c0-6ccb84420217","vaultid":"5deb4b09-51a2-4780-bb92-7a97ae4247c3","uId":"f97ff4ef-6b8f-4144-a6b5-982afdb06fee","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><figure class=\"table\"><table class=\"gdr\"><caption style=\"caption-side: top;\"><p style=\"text-align: center;\">Time Participants Spent Reading about Five London Museums</p></caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Museum Name</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Ranking</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Percentage of total time spent reading about museum by participants provided with ranking</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Percentage of total time spent reading about museum by participants not provided with ranking</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">British Museum</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">1</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">36</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">18</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">National Gallery</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">2</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">21</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">20</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Tate Modern</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">4</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">16</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">17</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Victoria and Albert Museum</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">5</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">14</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">23</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Natural History Museum</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">3</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">13</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">22</td></tr></tbody></table></figure></p>\n<p>Researchers recently conducted an experiment to understand how we use rankings to make decisions. They created a fictitious travel website describing five museums in London. Then, they invited two groups of participants, who had never visited the museums, to review the site and select the museum they would be most likely to visit. Meanwhile, the researchers tracked the amount of time each participant spent reading about each museum. For one group, the website ranked each museum, titling the page &ldquo;The Top 5 Museums in London.&rdquo; For the other group, the museums and their descriptions were not ranked. The researchers concluded that when reviewing ranked lists, we tend to focus on the top-ranked option.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes data in the table that support the researchers&rsquo; conclusion?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"430e6af9-2b1b-4417-a771-033acc192aa8","content":"<p>Participants who were not provided with a ranking of the museums spent roughly equal amounts of time reading about each museum.</p>"},{"id":"6dd4d30b-e3c7-4c79-aaca-11339b460705","content":"<p>Participants who were provided with a ranking of the museums spent disproportionately more time reading about the British museum.</p>"},{"id":"79ad97da-b8af-4bb4-9a6a-0fe6f3816870","content":"<p>Participants who were provided with a ranking of the museums spent the least amount of time reading about the Natural History Museum.</p>"},{"id":"df2c3610-ed05-4db8-81ba-7c9f1b946042","content":"<p>Participants who were not provided with a ranking of the museums spent the most time reading about the Victoria and Albert Museum.</p>"}],"keys":["6dd4d30b-e3c7-4c79-aaca-11339b460705"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. By looking at the top-ranked option, we can see that people provided with ranked lists spent more time reading about the British Museum than reading about other museums (36% of the time versus 21% for the second-ranked option).</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The claim is about people with ranked lists, and these data are about those with unranked lists. Choice C is incorrect. The claim is about people with ranked lists looking at the top-ranked option, and these data are about the third-ranked option. Choice D is incorrect. The claim is about people with ranked lists, and these data are about those with unranked lists.</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-QUA-E005","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"54d406f8-0ad6-47ab-8e00-4a071b0e2187","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959369,"updateDate":1691007959369}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959369, 1691007959369),
    ($SATQ$abbea24d$SATQ$, $SATQ$e24d7288-d58e-49e9-92dc-1ba13221b2e3$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>&ldquo;The Mountain&rdquo; is a 1914 poem by Robert Frost. In the poem, the speaker visits a town next to a mountain. The speaker claims to feel protected by the mountain, saying <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which quotation from &ldquo;The Mountain&rdquo; most effectively illustrates the claim?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"d7537a0b-6a47-4f4a-a521-39d61962da32","content":"<p>&ldquo;A dry ravine emerged under boughs / Into the pasture.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"85cdc3fe-a818-4826-b1f6-488ff8c949e7","content":"<p>&ldquo;The mountain stood there to be pointed at.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"88269161-89fb-4105-a7af-b9023b08cdff","content":"<p>&ldquo;I felt it like a wall / Behind which I was sheltered from a wind.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"3ec775c7-2ea0-4d82-8d63-142a76df3727","content":"<p>&ldquo;I crossed the river and swung round the mountain.&rdquo;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["88269161-89fb-4105-a7af-b9023b08cdff"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most effectively uses a quotation from "The Mountain" to illustrate the claim that the speaker feels protected by the mountain. In the quotation, the speaker recounts that the mountain felt like a wall that offers shelter from the wind. That the speaker felt sheltered by the mountain suggests that it offered the speaker a sense of being protected.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because this quotation doesn&rsquo;t express the speaker&rsquo;s feeling of being protected by the mountain. Instead, it notes that the speaker has seen a dry ravine and a pasture. Choice B is incorrect because this quotation doesn&rsquo;t express the speaker&rsquo;s feeling of being protected by the mountain. Instead, it simply states that there is a mountain, which is meant to be viewed. Choice D is incorrect because this quotation doesn&rsquo;t express the speaker&rsquo;s feeling of being protected by the mountain. Instead, it indicates that the speaker has gone around the mountain.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"abbea24d","external_id":"e24d7288-d58e-49e9-92dc-1ba13221b2e3","vaultid":null,"uId":"d9c81334-d653-48cb-876d-9940a7c0a0d3","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>&ldquo;The Mountain&rdquo; is a 1914 poem by Robert Frost. In the poem, the speaker visits a town next to a mountain. The speaker claims to feel protected by the mountain, saying <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which quotation from &ldquo;The Mountain&rdquo; most effectively illustrates the claim?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"d7537a0b-6a47-4f4a-a521-39d61962da32","content":"<p>&ldquo;A dry ravine emerged under boughs / Into the pasture.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"85cdc3fe-a818-4826-b1f6-488ff8c949e7","content":"<p>&ldquo;The mountain stood there to be pointed at.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"88269161-89fb-4105-a7af-b9023b08cdff","content":"<p>&ldquo;I felt it like a wall / Behind which I was sheltered from a wind.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"3ec775c7-2ea0-4d82-8d63-142a76df3727","content":"<p>&ldquo;I crossed the river and swung round the mountain.&rdquo;</p>"}],"keys":["88269161-89fb-4105-a7af-b9023b08cdff"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most effectively uses a quotation from \"The Mountain\" to illustrate the claim that the speaker feels protected by the mountain. In the quotation, the speaker recounts that the mountain felt like a wall that offers shelter from the wind. That the speaker felt sheltered by the mountain suggests that it offered the speaker a sense of being protected.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because this quotation doesn&rsquo;t express the speaker&rsquo;s feeling of being protected by the mountain. Instead, it notes that the speaker has seen a dry ravine and a pasture. Choice B is incorrect because this quotation doesn&rsquo;t express the speaker&rsquo;s feeling of being protected by the mountain. Instead, it simply states that there is a mountain, which is meant to be viewed. Choice D is incorrect because this quotation doesn&rsquo;t express the speaker&rsquo;s feeling of being protected by the mountain. Instead, it indicates that the speaker has gone around the mountain.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669214,"updateDate":1743430669214}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669214, 1743430669214),
    ($SATQ$ac285054$SATQ$, $SATQ$a63e4700-e95a-4a33-82f6-4f46c42d023e$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$69c21844-7111-44b5-aadf-bf317c7fdf2e$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$INF$SATQ$, $SATQ$Inferences$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The domestic sweet potato (<em>Ipomoea batatas</em>) descends from a wild plant native to South America. It also populates the Polynesian Islands, where evidence confirms that Native Hawaiians and other Indigenous peoples were cultivating the plant centuries before seafaring first occurred over the thousands of miles of ocean separating them from South America. To explain how the sweet potato was first introduced in Polynesia, botanist Pablo Mu&ntilde;oz-Rodr&iacute;guez and colleagues analyzed the DNA of numerous varieties of the plant, concluding that Polynesian varieties diverged from South American ones over 100,000 years ago. Given that Polynesia was peopled only in the last three thousand years, the team concluded that <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"5ba0c467-14c5-46d1-b1b6-ff846118c44b","content":"<p>the cultivation of the sweet potato<em>&nbsp;</em>in Polynesia likely predates its cultivation in South America.</p>"},{"id":"9b55718f-7657-4943-b4ba-48b533633edd","content":"<p>Polynesian peoples likely acquired the sweet potato from South American peoples only within the last three thousand years.</p>"},{"id":"cb72ea0d-60ba-46c5-aa8c-00a903a91ad7","content":"<p>human activity likely played no role in the introduction of the sweet potato in Polynesia.</p>"},{"id":"a64d4744-5900-4f19-b239-2f7ea3e36372","content":"<p>Polynesian sweet potato varieties likely descend from a single South American variety that was domesticated, not wild.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["cb72ea0d-60ba-46c5-aa8c-00a903a91ad7"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the sweet potato in Polynesia. The text indicates that the sweet potato is found in Polynesia but originated in South America, and that the sweet potato was being cultivated by Native Hawaiians and other Indigenous peoples in Polynesia long before sea voyages between South America and Polynesia began. The text goes on to note that research by Mu&ntilde;oz-Rodr&iacute;guez and colleagues has established that the Polynesian varieties of sweet potato split from South American varieties more than 100,000 years ago, which is thousands of years before humans settled in Polynesia. If Polynesian peoples were cultivating the sweet potato before sea voyages between Polynesia and South America began, and if Polynesian varieties of sweet potato diverged from South American varieties well before people were in Polynesia, it can reasonably be concluded that humans didn&rsquo;t play a role in bringing the sweet potato to Polynesia.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The text doesn&rsquo;t provide any information about when the sweet potato began to be cultivated in South America, so there&rsquo;s no support for the conclusion that cultivation began in Polynesia before it began in South America.&nbsp;Choice B is incorrect because the text indicates that the sweet potato was being cultivated in Polynesia long before sea journeys between Polynesia and South America began. Therefore, it wouldn&rsquo;t be reasonable to conclude that Polynesian peoples acquired the sweet potato from South American peoples. Additionally, the text indicates that the Polynesian varieties of sweet potato diverged from the South American varieties thousands of years before people settled in Polynesia, which suggests that the sweet potato was already present in Polynesia when people arrived.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because the text states that the domestic sweet potato, which is found in Polynesia, descends from a wild South American plant, not from a domesticated South American plant. The only people that the text describes as cultivating the sweet potato are Native Hawaiians and other Indigenous peoples of Polynesia.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-027-INF$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$32f63f71-c872-4fc3-9d7e-4e9df25d9db2$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"ac285054","external_id":"a63e4700-e95a-4a33-82f6-4f46c42d023e","vaultid":"69c21844-7111-44b5-aadf-bf317c7fdf2e","uId":"226dbff2-6013-4ec2-b6b8-d5f7de4ec822","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"INF","skill_desc":"Inferences","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The domestic sweet potato (<em>Ipomoea batatas</em>) descends from a wild plant native to South America. It also populates the Polynesian Islands, where evidence confirms that Native Hawaiians and other Indigenous peoples were cultivating the plant centuries before seafaring first occurred over the thousands of miles of ocean separating them from South America. To explain how the sweet potato was first introduced in Polynesia, botanist Pablo Mu&ntilde;oz-Rodr&iacute;guez and colleagues analyzed the DNA of numerous varieties of the plant, concluding that Polynesian varieties diverged from South American ones over 100,000 years ago. Given that Polynesia was peopled only in the last three thousand years, the team concluded that <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"5ba0c467-14c5-46d1-b1b6-ff846118c44b","content":"<p>the cultivation of the sweet potato<em>&nbsp;</em>in Polynesia likely predates its cultivation in South America.</p>"},{"id":"9b55718f-7657-4943-b4ba-48b533633edd","content":"<p>Polynesian peoples likely acquired the sweet potato from South American peoples only within the last three thousand years.</p>"},{"id":"cb72ea0d-60ba-46c5-aa8c-00a903a91ad7","content":"<p>human activity likely played no role in the introduction of the sweet potato in Polynesia.</p>"},{"id":"a64d4744-5900-4f19-b239-2f7ea3e36372","content":"<p>Polynesian sweet potato varieties likely descend from a single South American variety that was domesticated, not wild.</p>"}],"keys":["cb72ea0d-60ba-46c5-aa8c-00a903a91ad7"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the sweet potato in Polynesia. The text indicates that the sweet potato is found in Polynesia but originated in South America, and that the sweet potato was being cultivated by Native Hawaiians and other Indigenous peoples in Polynesia long before sea voyages between South America and Polynesia began. The text goes on to note that research by Mu&ntilde;oz-Rodr&iacute;guez and colleagues has established that the Polynesian varieties of sweet potato split from South American varieties more than 100,000 years ago, which is thousands of years before humans settled in Polynesia. If Polynesian peoples were cultivating the sweet potato before sea voyages between Polynesia and South America began, and if Polynesian varieties of sweet potato diverged from South American varieties well before people were in Polynesia, it can reasonably be concluded that humans didn&rsquo;t play a role in bringing the sweet potato to Polynesia.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The text doesn&rsquo;t provide any information about when the sweet potato began to be cultivated in South America, so there&rsquo;s no support for the conclusion that cultivation began in Polynesia before it began in South America.&nbsp;Choice B is incorrect because the text indicates that the sweet potato was being cultivated in Polynesia long before sea journeys between Polynesia and South America began. Therefore, it wouldn&rsquo;t be reasonable to conclude that Polynesian peoples acquired the sweet potato from South American peoples. Additionally, the text indicates that the Polynesian varieties of sweet potato diverged from the South American varieties thousands of years before people settled in Polynesia, which suggests that the sweet potato was already present in Polynesia when people arrived.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because the text states that the domestic sweet potato, which is found in Polynesia, descends from a wild South American plant, not from a domesticated South American plant. The only people that the text describes as cultivating the sweet potato are Native Hawaiians and other Indigenous peoples of Polynesia.&nbsp;</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-027-INF","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"32f63f71-c872-4fc3-9d7e-4e9df25d9db2","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959318,"updateDate":1691007959318}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959318, 1691007959318),
    ($SATQ$ac7166f7$SATQ$, $SATQ$6dce1c90-e8b3-47f8-875b-54b98618cf7d$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Researchers Narelle Haworth and Amy Schramm studied bicycling behavior in Queensland, Australia. Haworth and Schramm asked adult bike riders questions about their level of experience, reasons for riding a bike, and route preferences. The researchers claim that <span style="text-decoration: underline;" role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content">experienced riders who mainly bike to work tend to prefer routes that reduce their travel time</span>.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which survey response from a bike rider in Queensland would best support the underlined claim?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"4ba37718-8462-488e-ad95-05ba150e8520","content":"<p>&ldquo;I have a bike, but I don&rsquo;t ride it very often. When the weather is nice, I sometimes use my bike to go into town to do some shopping.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"51956b2e-0d56-4524-98d4-32aba9d81c86","content":"<p>&ldquo;I just got a new bike, and I&rsquo;m looking forward to going on rides with my friends soon.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"7298b2d9-fe86-4bc3-80e8-0ca865f2112f","content":"<p>&ldquo;I bike to my job every day. There&rsquo;s a nice bike path that goes through a park, but I use the bike lane on the main road because it&rsquo;s faster.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"cc445c72-f376-4fb6-8c5e-abe386941b66","content":"<p>&ldquo;I usually bike to work, but I&rsquo;m taking the bus now because my bike has a broken part that needs to be fixed.&rdquo;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["7298b2d9-fe86-4bc3-80e8-0ca865f2112f"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it presents a response that best supports the underlined claim that experienced riders who mainly bike to work tend to prefer routes that reduce their travel time. The bike rider responds that they bike to work every day and that they use the bike lane because it is faster; thus, it is reasonable to assume that they are an experienced rider who prefers routes that reduce their travel time to work.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because this response does not address the claim. The claim is about experienced riders preferring to take routes that reduce their travel time on the way to work; however, this response is from an inexperienced rider who is discussing biking for personal reasons. Choice B is incorrect because this response is not related to the underlined claim. The response from this rider does not establish the level of experience they have with riding, nor does it state that the rider uses a bike to get to work. Choice D is incorrect. Although this response is from an experienced rider who mainly bikes to work, the response also mentions that the rider&rsquo;s bike is broken, which is not related to any route preference.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"ac7166f7","external_id":"6dce1c90-e8b3-47f8-875b-54b98618cf7d","vaultid":null,"uId":"9b855e71-aa52-49c8-9710-a36e1d9b4d3b","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Researchers Narelle Haworth and Amy Schramm studied bicycling behavior in Queensland, Australia. Haworth and Schramm asked adult bike riders questions about their level of experience, reasons for riding a bike, and route preferences. The researchers claim that <span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\">experienced riders who mainly bike to work tend to prefer routes that reduce their travel time</span>.</p>","stem":"<p>Which survey response from a bike rider in Queensland would best support the underlined claim?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"4ba37718-8462-488e-ad95-05ba150e8520","content":"<p>&ldquo;I have a bike, but I don&rsquo;t ride it very often. When the weather is nice, I sometimes use my bike to go into town to do some shopping.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"51956b2e-0d56-4524-98d4-32aba9d81c86","content":"<p>&ldquo;I just got a new bike, and I&rsquo;m looking forward to going on rides with my friends soon.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"7298b2d9-fe86-4bc3-80e8-0ca865f2112f","content":"<p>&ldquo;I bike to my job every day. There&rsquo;s a nice bike path that goes through a park, but I use the bike lane on the main road because it&rsquo;s faster.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"cc445c72-f376-4fb6-8c5e-abe386941b66","content":"<p>&ldquo;I usually bike to work, but I&rsquo;m taking the bus now because my bike has a broken part that needs to be fixed.&rdquo;</p>"}],"keys":["7298b2d9-fe86-4bc3-80e8-0ca865f2112f"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it presents a response that best supports the underlined claim that experienced riders who mainly bike to work tend to prefer routes that reduce their travel time. The bike rider responds that they bike to work every day and that they use the bike lane because it is faster; thus, it is reasonable to assume that they are an experienced rider who prefers routes that reduce their travel time to work.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because this response does not address the claim. The claim is about experienced riders preferring to take routes that reduce their travel time on the way to work; however, this response is from an inexperienced rider who is discussing biking for personal reasons. Choice B is incorrect because this response is not related to the underlined claim. The response from this rider does not establish the level of experience they have with riding, nor does it state that the rider uses a bike to get to work. Choice D is incorrect. Although this response is from an experienced rider who mainly bikes to work, the response also mentions that the rider&rsquo;s bike is broken, which is not related to any route preference.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323045,"updateDate":1730147323045}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323045, 1730147323045),
    ($SATQ$ad680167$SATQ$, $SATQ$7073bd45-271c-4b31-b606-013a65b61485$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$ff0b971e-1700-4e0b-b35f-bb2caae5a759$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$CID$SATQ$, $SATQ$Central Ideas and Details$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The recovery of a 1,000-year-old Chinese shipwreck in the Java Sea near present-day Indonesia has yielded a treasure trove of artifacts, including thousands of small ceramic bowls. Using a portable X-ray fluorescence analyzer tool, Lisa Niziolek and her team were able to detect the chemical composition of these bowls without damaging them. By comparing the chemical signatures of the bowls with those of the materials still at old Chinese kiln sites, Niziolek and her team can pinpoint which Chinese kilns likely produced the ceramic bowls.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best states the main idea of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"de260c18-52b9-4b1c-bf6b-20ee8f550720","content":"<p>Because of a new technology, researchers can locate and recover more shipwrecks than they could in the past.</p>"},{"id":"a1d16a99-c60d-4e2a-a293-37f6687b3771","content":"<p>Researchers have been able to identify the location of a number of Chinese kilns in operation 1,000 years ago.</p>"},{"id":"4db88ee2-36e7-4532-b9ce-ef34cd0fff74","content":"<p>With the help of a special tool, researchers have determined the likely origin of bowls recovered from a shipwreck.</p>"},{"id":"1ceacce2-f580-4607-aad0-526045ffe36f","content":"<p>Before the invention of portable X-ray fluorescence, researchers needed to take a small piece out of an artifact to analyze its components.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["4db88ee2-36e7-4532-b9ce-ef34cd0fff74"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most accurately states the main idea of the text. According to the text, thousands of ceramic bowls were found in a recovered Chinese shipwreck. The text goes on to say that Niziolek and her team used a special tool, a portable X-ray fluorescence analyzer, to determine the bowls&rsquo; chemical signatures. Comparing these chemical signatures with the chemical signatures of materials they had collected from old Chinese kiln sites, the text says, allowed the researchers to identify which kilns had produced the bowls. In other words, the researchers determined the bowls&rsquo; origin. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although the text indicates that the researchers used technology in the form of a portable X-ray fluorescence analyzer, it doesn&rsquo;t specifically state that this technology is new. In addition, the text says that Niziolek and her team used the tool to determine the chemical composition of bowls that were found in a Chinese shipwreck, not to locate and recover the shipwreck itself. There&rsquo;s no indication in the text that a new technology can help researchers locate and recover shipwrecks. Choice B is incorrect because the text indicates that the researchers collected materials from old kiln sites for chemical comparison with the ceramic bowls, which means that the researchers must have already known the location of those kiln sites. Rather than identifying the location of the kilns, the researchers determined which kilns in operation 1,000 years ago had likely produced the bowls that were found in the shipwreck.
&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect. Although the text says that using a portable X-ray fluorescence analyzer tool enabled Niziolek and her team to analyze artifacts in the form of ceramic bowls without damaging them, the text doesn&rsquo;t discuss how researchers analyzed artifacts before this tool was invented. Moreover, the point that the bowls were left undamaged isn&rsquo;t the text&rsquo;s main idea. Rather, it&rsquo;s a detail that&rsquo;s provided to develop the main idea, which is that the researchers used a special tool to determine where the bowls had been produced. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-036-CID$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$b7415e1a-30a2-476c-bdd0-eeac906df9e6$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"ad680167","external_id":"7073bd45-271c-4b31-b606-013a65b61485","vaultid":"ff0b971e-1700-4e0b-b35f-bb2caae5a759","uId":"9c5f02ac-8643-4375-8259-fcf595d26d93","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"CID","skill_desc":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The recovery of a 1,000-year-old Chinese shipwreck in the Java Sea near present-day Indonesia has yielded a treasure trove of artifacts, including thousands of small ceramic bowls. Using a portable X-ray fluorescence analyzer tool, Lisa Niziolek and her team were able to detect the chemical composition of these bowls without damaging them. By comparing the chemical signatures of the bowls with those of the materials still at old Chinese kiln sites, Niziolek and her team can pinpoint which Chinese kilns likely produced the ceramic bowls.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best states the main idea of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"de260c18-52b9-4b1c-bf6b-20ee8f550720","content":"<p>Because of a new technology, researchers can locate and recover more shipwrecks than they could in the past.</p>"},{"id":"a1d16a99-c60d-4e2a-a293-37f6687b3771","content":"<p>Researchers have been able to identify the location of a number of Chinese kilns in operation 1,000 years ago.</p>"},{"id":"4db88ee2-36e7-4532-b9ce-ef34cd0fff74","content":"<p>With the help of a special tool, researchers have determined the likely origin of bowls recovered from a shipwreck.</p>"},{"id":"1ceacce2-f580-4607-aad0-526045ffe36f","content":"<p>Before the invention of portable X-ray fluorescence, researchers needed to take a small piece out of an artifact to analyze its components.</p>"}],"keys":["4db88ee2-36e7-4532-b9ce-ef34cd0fff74"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most accurately states the main idea of the text. According to the text, thousands of ceramic bowls were found in a recovered Chinese shipwreck. The text goes on to say that Niziolek and her team used a special tool, a portable X-ray fluorescence analyzer, to determine the bowls&rsquo; chemical signatures. Comparing these chemical signatures with the chemical signatures of materials they had collected from old Chinese kiln sites, the text says, allowed the researchers to identify which kilns had produced the bowls. In other words, the researchers determined the bowls&rsquo; origin. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although the text indicates that the researchers used technology in the form of a portable X-ray fluorescence analyzer, it doesn&rsquo;t specifically state that this technology is new. In addition, the text says that Niziolek and her team used the tool to determine the chemical composition of bowls that were found in a Chinese shipwreck, not to locate and recover the shipwreck itself. There&rsquo;s no indication in the text that a new technology can help researchers locate and recover shipwrecks. Choice B is incorrect because the text indicates that the researchers collected materials from old kiln sites for chemical comparison with the ceramic bowls, which means that the researchers must have already known the location of those kiln sites. Rather than identifying the location of the kilns, the researchers determined which kilns in operation 1,000 years ago had likely produced the bowls that were found in the shipwreck.\n&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect. Although the text says that using a portable X-ray fluorescence analyzer tool enabled Niziolek and her team to analyze artifacts in the form of ceramic bowls without damaging them, the text doesn&rsquo;t discuss how researchers analyzed artifacts before this tool was invented. Moreover, the point that the bowls were left undamaged isn&rsquo;t the text&rsquo;s main idea. Rather, it&rsquo;s a detail that&rsquo;s provided to develop the main idea, which is that the researchers used a special tool to determine where the bowls had been produced. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-036-CID","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"b7415e1a-30a2-476c-bdd0-eeac906df9e6","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959320,"updateDate":1691007959320}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959320, 1691007959320),
    ($SATQ$adbcbce0$SATQ$, $SATQ$57e0927e-845b-410d-a414-0ada4098e9b8$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$f75f99ec-f130-4de5-87dc-c0ccccf617b3$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$CID$SATQ$, $SATQ$Central Ideas and Details$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is adapted from Christina Rossetti&rsquo;s 1881 poem &ldquo;Monna Innominata 2.&rdquo;</p>
<p>I wish I could remember that first day,</p>
<p>&ensp;First hour, first moment of your meeting me,</p>
<p>&ensp;If bright or dim the season, it might be</p>
<p>Summer or Winter for [all] I can say;</p>
<p>So unrecorded did it slip away,</p>
<p>&ensp;So blind was I to see and to foresee,</p>
<p>&ensp;So dull to mark the budding of my tree</p>
<p>That would not blossom yet for many a May.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best states the main idea of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"d9dad6dc-28f8-4b96-b237-59b6973783e7","content":"<p>The speaker celebrates how the passage of time has strengthened a relationship that once seemed unimportant.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"047bcdba-f431-444e-b571-3dfebedf45f9","content":"<p>Because the speaker did not anticipate how important a relationship would become, she cannot recall how the relationship began, which she regrets.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"5c68f126-0320-4125-b04f-511ace6b4d93","content":"<p>As the anniversary of the beginning of an important relationship approaches, the speaker feels conflicted about how best to commemorate it.</p>"},{"id":"0aba0e31-956b-457f-9990-824bd14eb2f5","content":"<p>After years of neglecting a once valuable relationship, the speaker worries it may be too late for her to salvage the relationship.&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["047bcdba-f431-444e-b571-3dfebedf45f9"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The speaker says that they wish they could remember when they first met someone, but they can&rsquo;t remember the meeting at all, because they didn&rsquo;t know at the time that the relationship would "blossom" later on.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The speaker does say that the relationship has "blossomed" over time, but only briefly at the end&mdash;and they don&rsquo;t really "celebrate" that fact. Rather, the text has a more regretful tone: it&rsquo;s about how the speaker can&rsquo;t remember first meeting this person, and they wish they could. Choice C is incorrect. The speaker doesn&rsquo;t mention an anniversary&mdash;in fact, the speaker can&rsquo;t remember when they met the person they&rsquo;re talking about. Choice D is incorrect. The speaker doesn&rsquo;t say that they neglected the relationship. In fact, the speaker suggests that the relationship has become very important to them&mdash;that&rsquo;s why the speaker wishes that they could remember their first meeting.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-CID-M005$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$9319767c-28a2-41ac-8330-5c7e57dd30c9$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"adbcbce0","external_id":"57e0927e-845b-410d-a414-0ada4098e9b8","vaultid":"f75f99ec-f130-4de5-87dc-c0ccccf617b3","uId":"a0b641fa-e10a-4e4d-9b36-a30f30b1e90a","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"CID","skill_desc":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is adapted from Christina Rossetti&rsquo;s 1881 poem &ldquo;Monna Innominata 2.&rdquo;</p>\n<p>I wish I could remember that first day,</p>\n<p>&ensp;First hour, first moment of your meeting me,</p>\n<p>&ensp;If bright or dim the season, it might be</p>\n<p>Summer or Winter for [all] I can say;</p>\n<p>So unrecorded did it slip away,</p>\n<p>&ensp;So blind was I to see and to foresee,</p>\n<p>&ensp;So dull to mark the budding of my tree</p>\n<p>That would not blossom yet for many a May.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best states the main idea of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"d9dad6dc-28f8-4b96-b237-59b6973783e7","content":"<p>The speaker celebrates how the passage of time has strengthened a relationship that once seemed unimportant.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"047bcdba-f431-444e-b571-3dfebedf45f9","content":"<p>Because the speaker did not anticipate how important a relationship would become, she cannot recall how the relationship began, which she regrets.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"5c68f126-0320-4125-b04f-511ace6b4d93","content":"<p>As the anniversary of the beginning of an important relationship approaches, the speaker feels conflicted about how best to commemorate it.</p>"},{"id":"0aba0e31-956b-457f-9990-824bd14eb2f5","content":"<p>After years of neglecting a once valuable relationship, the speaker worries it may be too late for her to salvage the relationship.&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["047bcdba-f431-444e-b571-3dfebedf45f9"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The speaker says that they wish they could remember when they first met someone, but they can&rsquo;t remember the meeting at all, because they didn&rsquo;t know at the time that the relationship would \"blossom\" later on.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The speaker does say that the relationship has \"blossomed\" over time, but only briefly at the end&mdash;and they don&rsquo;t really \"celebrate\" that fact. Rather, the text has a more regretful tone: it&rsquo;s about how the speaker can&rsquo;t remember first meeting this person, and they wish they could. Choice C is incorrect. The speaker doesn&rsquo;t mention an anniversary&mdash;in fact, the speaker can&rsquo;t remember when they met the person they&rsquo;re talking about. Choice D is incorrect. The speaker doesn&rsquo;t say that they neglected the relationship. In fact, the speaker suggests that the relationship has become very important to them&mdash;that&rsquo;s why the speaker wishes that they could remember their first meeting.</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-CID-M005","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"9319767c-28a2-41ac-8330-5c7e57dd30c9","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959360,"updateDate":1691007959360}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959360, 1691007959360),
    ($SATQ$af125459$SATQ$, $SATQ$5ad79e33-9613-4f29-9933-9caaa3d9a62d$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$2c3ae76c-9b83-4b98-aacc-8cf0943a9662$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><figure class="table"><table class="gdr"><caption style="caption-side: top;"><p style="text-align: center;">Number and Origin of Clamshell Tools Found at Different Depths below the Surface in Neanderthal Cave</p></caption><thead><tr><th scope="col" style="text-align: center; vertical-align: bottom;">Depth of tools found below surface in cave (meters)</th><th scope="col" style="text-align: center; vertical-align: bottom;">Clamshells that Neanderthals collected from the beach</th><th scope="col" style="text-align: center; vertical-align: bottom;">Clamshells that Neanderthals harvested from the seafloor</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: center;">2&ndash;3</th><td style="text-align: center;">7</td><td style="text-align: center;">0</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: center;">3&ndash;4</th><td style="text-align: center;">99</td><td style="text-align: center;">33</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: center;">4&ndash;5</th><td style="text-align: center;">2</td><td style="text-align: center;">0</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: center;">5&ndash;6</th><td style="text-align: center;">18</td><td style="text-align: center;">7</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: center;">6&ndash;7</th><td style="text-align: center;">1</td><td style="text-align: center;">0</td></tr></tbody></table></figure></p>
<p>Two kinds of clamshell tools used by Neanderthals were dug up in a cave on the western coast of Italy. Archaeologist Paola Villa and her colleagues studied the tools and determined that Neanderthals either collected clams that had washed onto the beach or harvested clams from the seafloor and then sharpened the shells to make tools. The highest number of tools made from clamshells that were collected from the beach was found at a depth of <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"e6de0450-ffc4-4c5e-a257-09d51388289f","content":"<p>5&ndash;6 meters below the surface.</p>"},{"id":"ddce950e-2991-43a3-935f-c37166d294ab","content":"<p>4&ndash;5 meters below the surface.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"d46fe3e7-d5d8-43be-9211-a9fb29423717","content":"<p>3&ndash;4 meters below the surface.</p>"},{"id":"b385c2b2-b9be-4ab3-97eb-289bfec332b1","content":"<p>6&ndash;7 meters below the surface.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["d46fe3e7-d5d8-43be-9211-a9fb29423717"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement about the depth at which the highest number of tools made from clamshells that Neanderthals collected from the beach was found. The table presents the depths at which Neanderthal clamshell tools were found, and, for each depth, the number of those tools made from clamshells that washed up on the beach and the number made from clamshells harvested from the seafloor. The table indicates that the highest number made from clamshells collected from the beach was 99 and that these tools were found at a depth of 3&ndash;4 meters. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the table indicates that 18 tools made from clamshells collected from the beach were found at a depth of 5&ndash;6 meters, which is fewer than the 99 tools found at a depth of 3&ndash;4 meters. Choice B is incorrect because the table indicates that 2 tools made from clamshells collected from the beach were found at a depth of 4&ndash;5 meters, which is fewer than the 99 tools found at a depth of 3&ndash;4 meters. Choice D is incorrect because the table indicates that 1 tool made from clamshells collected from the beach was found at a depth of 6&ndash;7 meters, which is fewer than the 99 tools found at a depth of 3&ndash;4 meters. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-530-QUA$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$79ebe409-7a3c-436a-87ee-b85e5a0cc6e3$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"af125459","external_id":"5ad79e33-9613-4f29-9933-9caaa3d9a62d","vaultid":"2c3ae76c-9b83-4b98-aacc-8cf0943a9662","uId":"eda584b6-c6ce-4c0a-86a3-0636d37c4bb5","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><figure class=\"table\"><table class=\"gdr\"><caption style=\"caption-side: top;\"><p style=\"text-align: center;\">Number and Origin of Clamshell Tools Found at Different Depths below the Surface in Neanderthal Cave</p></caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center; vertical-align: bottom;\">Depth of tools found below surface in cave (meters)</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center; vertical-align: bottom;\">Clamshells that Neanderthals collected from the beach</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center; vertical-align: bottom;\">Clamshells that Neanderthals harvested from the seafloor</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: center;\">2&ndash;3</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">7</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">0</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: center;\">3&ndash;4</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">99</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">33</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: center;\">4&ndash;5</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">2</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">0</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: center;\">5&ndash;6</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">18</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">7</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: center;\">6&ndash;7</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">1</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">0</td></tr></tbody></table></figure></p>\n<p>Two kinds of clamshell tools used by Neanderthals were dug up in a cave on the western coast of Italy. Archaeologist Paola Villa and her colleagues studied the tools and determined that Neanderthals either collected clams that had washed onto the beach or harvested clams from the seafloor and then sharpened the shells to make tools. The highest number of tools made from clamshells that were collected from the beach was found at a depth of <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"e6de0450-ffc4-4c5e-a257-09d51388289f","content":"<p>5&ndash;6 meters below the surface.</p>"},{"id":"ddce950e-2991-43a3-935f-c37166d294ab","content":"<p>4&ndash;5 meters below the surface.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"d46fe3e7-d5d8-43be-9211-a9fb29423717","content":"<p>3&ndash;4 meters below the surface.</p>"},{"id":"b385c2b2-b9be-4ab3-97eb-289bfec332b1","content":"<p>6&ndash;7 meters below the surface.</p>"}],"keys":["d46fe3e7-d5d8-43be-9211-a9fb29423717"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement about the depth at which the highest number of tools made from clamshells that Neanderthals collected from the beach was found. The table presents the depths at which Neanderthal clamshell tools were found, and, for each depth, the number of those tools made from clamshells that washed up on the beach and the number made from clamshells harvested from the seafloor. The table indicates that the highest number made from clamshells collected from the beach was 99 and that these tools were found at a depth of 3&ndash;4 meters. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the table indicates that 18 tools made from clamshells collected from the beach were found at a depth of 5&ndash;6 meters, which is fewer than the 99 tools found at a depth of 3&ndash;4 meters. Choice B is incorrect because the table indicates that 2 tools made from clamshells collected from the beach were found at a depth of 4&ndash;5 meters, which is fewer than the 99 tools found at a depth of 3&ndash;4 meters. Choice D is incorrect because the table indicates that 1 tool made from clamshells collected from the beach was found at a depth of 6&ndash;7 meters, which is fewer than the 99 tools found at a depth of 3&ndash;4 meters. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-530-QUA","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"79ebe409-7a3c-436a-87ee-b85e5a0cc6e3","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959338,"updateDate":1691007959338}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959338, 1691007959338),
    ($SATQ$af9e3240$SATQ$, $SATQ$be347d28-4be3-4fe4-b8ec-28dab78f77a8$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$1da7b323-c8ca-4601-a4a4-6b73cc010204$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><em>Electra</em> is a circa 420&ndash;410 BCE play by Sophocles, translated in 1870 by R.C. Jebb. Electra, who is in mourning for her dead father and her long-absent brother, is aware of the intensity of her grief but believes it to be justified: <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which quotation from <em>Electra</em> most effectively illustrates the claim?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"5587348b-9565-4d2b-a0e0-c4fbc1a2bc7c","content":"<p>&ldquo;O thou pure sunlight, and thou air, earth&rsquo;s canopy, how often have ye heard the strains of my lament, the wild blows dealt against this bleeding breast, when dark night fails!&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"381b4f29-f3ad-4fa2-accd-7eac5d648c32","content":"<p>&ldquo;Send to me my brother; for I have no more the strength to bear up alone against the load of grief that weighs me down.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"668b8610-b15f-494d-a812-ecbcac1e3ee6","content":"<p>&ldquo;I know my&nbsp;own passion, it escapes me not; but, seeing that the causes are so dire,&nbsp;will never curb these frenzied plaints, while life is in me.&rdquo;&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"6c0c4855-b314-4083-95e0-766f56e6139f","content":"<p>&ldquo;But never will I cease from dirge and sore lament, while I look on the trembling rays of the bright stars, or on this light of day.&rdquo;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["668b8610-b15f-494d-a812-ecbcac1e3ee6"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. Electra states that she &ldquo;knows her own passion,&rdquo; which shows that she&rsquo;s aware of the intensity of her grief. But she also claims that the &ldquo;causes are so dire&rdquo;&mdash;meaning the reasons for her grief are so awful&mdash;that she can&rsquo;t let it go, which shows that she believes her grief is justified. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This quotation doesn&rsquo;t show that Electra believes her grief is justified. It shows that Electra is aware of its intensity, but it doesn&rsquo;t suggest that she believes she has a legitimate reason for feeling that way. Choice B is incorrect. This quotation doesn&rsquo;t show that Electra believes her grief is justified. It shows that Electra is aware of its intensity, but it doesn&rsquo;t suggest that she believes she has a legitimate reason for feeling that way. Choice D is incorrect. This quotation doesn&rsquo;t show that Electra believes her grief is justified. It shows that Electra is aware of the intensity of her grief, but it doesn&rsquo;t suggest that she has a legitimate reason for feeling that way. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-TXT-H001$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$03a847dd-3abf-4957-9594-3cb376b92e5d$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"af9e3240","external_id":"be347d28-4be3-4fe4-b8ec-28dab78f77a8","vaultid":"1da7b323-c8ca-4601-a4a4-6b73cc010204","uId":"9ce92aef-ebfc-4f2c-83c3-457673602961","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><em>Electra</em> is a circa 420&ndash;410 BCE play by Sophocles, translated in 1870 by R.C. Jebb. Electra, who is in mourning for her dead father and her long-absent brother, is aware of the intensity of her grief but believes it to be justified: <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which quotation from <em>Electra</em> most effectively illustrates the claim?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"5587348b-9565-4d2b-a0e0-c4fbc1a2bc7c","content":"<p>&ldquo;O thou pure sunlight, and thou air, earth&rsquo;s canopy, how often have ye heard the strains of my lament, the wild blows dealt against this bleeding breast, when dark night fails!&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"381b4f29-f3ad-4fa2-accd-7eac5d648c32","content":"<p>&ldquo;Send to me my brother; for I have no more the strength to bear up alone against the load of grief that weighs me down.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"668b8610-b15f-494d-a812-ecbcac1e3ee6","content":"<p>&ldquo;I know my&nbsp;own passion, it escapes me not; but, seeing that the causes are so dire,&nbsp;will never curb these frenzied plaints, while life is in me.&rdquo;&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"6c0c4855-b314-4083-95e0-766f56e6139f","content":"<p>&ldquo;But never will I cease from dirge and sore lament, while I look on the trembling rays of the bright stars, or on this light of day.&rdquo;</p>"}],"keys":["668b8610-b15f-494d-a812-ecbcac1e3ee6"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. Electra states that she &ldquo;knows her own passion,&rdquo; which shows that she&rsquo;s aware of the intensity of her grief. But she also claims that the &ldquo;causes are so dire&rdquo;&mdash;meaning the reasons for her grief are so awful&mdash;that she can&rsquo;t let it go, which shows that she believes her grief is justified. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This quotation doesn&rsquo;t show that Electra believes her grief is justified. It shows that Electra is aware of its intensity, but it doesn&rsquo;t suggest that she believes she has a legitimate reason for feeling that way. Choice B is incorrect. This quotation doesn&rsquo;t show that Electra believes her grief is justified. It shows that Electra is aware of its intensity, but it doesn&rsquo;t suggest that she believes she has a legitimate reason for feeling that way. Choice D is incorrect. This quotation doesn&rsquo;t show that Electra believes her grief is justified. It shows that Electra is aware of the intensity of her grief, but it doesn&rsquo;t suggest that she has a legitimate reason for feeling that way. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-TXT-H001","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"03a847dd-3abf-4957-9594-3cb376b92e5d","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959376,"updateDate":1691007959376}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959376, 1691007959376),
